WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:02.902
-Our next guest is a very
talented actor and comedian.

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:04.737
Comedian -- he's one of
the best comedians.

00:00:04.737 --> 00:00:07.239
He's the host of
the "WTF" podcast

00:00:07.239 --> 00:00:09.476
and stars in the new film
"Stardust,"

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:12.745
which is in theaters and on
demand starting November 25th.

00:00:12.745 --> 00:00:14.881
Here is Marc Maron.

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:16.883
Marc, look at you.
-How are you, man?

00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:18.918
-You look great.
-I'm outside, Jimmy.

00:00:18.918 --> 00:00:20.887
I'm outside.
-That's what I'm talking about.

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:22.889
You're outside.
All right, all right.

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:25.024
-All right, already.
What are we doing?

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:26.892
-I love that you're outside.
-...doing outside?

00:00:26.892 --> 00:00:28.461
I got the trees here.
-I would do the same thing.

00:00:28.461 --> 00:00:29.895
I would do the same thing.

00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:31.196
-I'll tell you, I miss
something, though,

00:00:31.196 --> 00:00:32.698
about being in this --

00:00:32.698 --> 00:00:34.401
I don't know how you're
handling this,

00:00:34.401 --> 00:00:36.769
but one of the things I used to
enjoy about doing the show

00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:39.005
is in the dressing room
you had the nice cookies

00:00:39.005 --> 00:00:40.707
and maybe a t-shirt.
Now are they being sent to me?

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:42.474
Because that's one
of the reasons --

00:00:42.474 --> 00:00:44.443
it's not the only reason
I do it,

00:00:44.443 --> 00:00:45.712
but am I getting the cookies?

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:47.446
-Of course you are.
-Thank you.

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:50.283
Because I don't know how this is
going to go without the cookies.

00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:52.885
I like to be jammed on sugar
every time I talk to you

00:00:52.885 --> 00:00:54.487
so I can keep up.

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:57.190
-Well, Marc, it's been a long
week for everybody.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:00.026
But you --
-Oh my God, thank God!

00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:01.460
Right?
I mean, jeez.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.029
I thought I was going to have to
leave the country,

00:01:03.029 --> 00:01:04.864
and that's not easy now.
I had a big plan.

00:01:04.864 --> 00:01:07.133
But if you want to get out,
you can't. You know why?

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:09.469
Because, we're plague-infected
pig people

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:11.737
who can't behave like
responsible adults.

00:01:11.737 --> 00:01:13.840
You can't even go
to another country.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.274
It's like, "I'm from America,

00:01:15.274 --> 00:01:16.943
I'd like to spend a year
in Ireland."

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:19.312
"Sorry, you plague-infected
pig person

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:22.047
who can't act like a responsible
grown-up."

00:01:22.047 --> 00:01:24.283
"But I have my mask."
"Well, tell that to the other

00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:26.852
pig people that live
in your country.

00:01:26.852 --> 00:01:29.689
You plague ridden monster."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:33.192
"Look at you, you plague-
infected pig person."

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:34.527
[ Laughter ]

00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:36.295
So, I'm glad that I can
stay in the country

00:01:36.295 --> 00:01:40.266
because I would've had to, like,
you know, mail myself somewhere.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:42.134
Just for a little while.
-But you got out.

00:01:42.134 --> 00:01:43.469
You said,
"I'm not watching this.

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:45.405
I got to get away somewhere."

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:47.440
-Oh, I did, yeah, for the actual
election, I'm like,

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.942
"What's the point of maps
and numbers for three days

00:01:49.942 --> 00:01:51.710
that aren't going to go
anywhere, but sit there

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.846
and make you go like,
'Oh, my God!

00:01:53.846 --> 00:01:55.814
Is he going to win?'"
-Yeah.

00:01:55.814 --> 00:01:59.685
-So, I went to up to Big Sur,
and I went to a hotel

00:01:59.685 --> 00:02:03.455
where there was spotty wi-fi,
spotty cell coverage,

00:02:03.455 --> 00:02:06.359
just me alone with my thoughts,

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:08.360
and it was nice to really
experience

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.630
the pure existential panic
and fear

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.231
without being interrupted by

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:14.534
televised existential
panic and fear.

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:15.768
It was surprising.

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:17.470
The small amount of information
I was getting

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.606
was enough for me to freak out
without any input whatsoever.

00:02:20.606 --> 00:02:22.308
So, I was there looking
at the ocean,

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:25.945
just sitting there going like,
"Oh, man, we're in trouble, man.

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:28.147
This is not going well,
but it's pretty.

00:02:28.147 --> 00:02:30.383
Look at the birds."
-Yeah.

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:34.586
-So a couple of sweaty nights
of panic but no TV.

00:02:34.586 --> 00:02:38.024
And I got home right in time
yesterday to catch

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:40.626
what was the turning point,
which was very exciting.

00:02:40.626 --> 00:02:42.428
And, I guess they voted out
COVID too,

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:44.096
no more COVID, which is nice.

00:02:44.096 --> 00:02:46.498
That's a relief, because I was
getting tired of that.

00:02:46.498 --> 00:02:48.501
-Next thing they
have to vote out

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:51.638
is quarantine sourdough bread.

00:02:51.638 --> 00:02:53.472
-Dude, I think that's over.

00:02:53.472 --> 00:02:56.075
I saw that kind of trickle away
within three months.

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:59.012
You got these like, hipster dads
and cute young moms like,

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:00.313
"Look at the bread I made."

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:02.548
But now they're just like,
"Where I bury my family?"

00:03:02.548 --> 00:03:05.418
"I couldn't -- I --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:08.521
The bread didn't hold me back.

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:11.057
Enough with the bread, what do I
do with the bodies?"

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:14.127
-That was all fake.
[ Laughing ]

00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:16.128
It was covering up,
yeah, something deep.

00:03:16.128 --> 00:03:17.730
-Yeah. Oh, yeah.
Just fury.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:20.900
Just like, "This will be fun.
Look, dad's making bread."

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.303
Three months later,
"Dad's a monster."

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:24.770
-Dad's getting a divorce.
-"Make some more bread."

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:27.573
"The bread's over.
Give me some candy."

00:03:27.573 --> 00:03:31.010
-So, I take it you haven't done
any baking, then?

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.745
-Well, you know,
I do enjoy cooking.

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:35.014
I'm shucking my own oysters.

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:36.982
And that's not code.
Well, I guess it is,

00:03:36.982 --> 00:03:39.686
'cause I'm doing that too.
But I'm actually --

00:03:39.686 --> 00:03:40.853
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:42.454
I'm actually --
-Oh, my gosh.

00:03:42.454 --> 00:03:44.256
-I'm actually --
-Are you really?

00:03:44.256 --> 00:03:46.058
-I buy oysters
and I shuck them.

00:03:46.058 --> 00:03:47.827
I learned how to shuck them
for the -- you know,

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:49.295
I'm going to need to know
that in the future.

00:03:49.295 --> 00:03:50.897
-Do you wear a chain glove?

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:52.932
-No, no, no. I watched a video.
I'm getting better at it.

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:55.434
But I like to eat the oysters.

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:57.636
I do baking.
I enjoy -- like I got it

00:03:57.636 --> 00:03:59.706
in my head last week --
a couple weeks ago,

00:03:59.706 --> 00:04:01.474
I'm going to make this cake.

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:03.810
'Cause I have the
"New York Times" cooking app,

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.713
or the Instagram
for "New York Times" cooking,

00:04:06.713 --> 00:04:08.281
and cakes will come through.

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:10.616
And one day, I'm just sort of
like, "Aw, man, life sucks.

00:04:10.616 --> 00:04:13.752
I'm going to make that cake,
and I put a lot of work into it.

00:04:13.752 --> 00:04:15.421
Got it -- You need a Bundt pan,

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:17.823
and you've got to poke holes
in it to pour the butter into.

00:04:17.823 --> 00:04:20.059
It was a complicated bit of
business,

00:04:20.059 --> 00:04:22.595
and I screwed up the last step.
And I knew it --

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:24.363
-Yep.
-As I did it, like,

00:04:24.363 --> 00:04:26.032
I screwed up the cake.

00:04:26.032 --> 00:04:27.867
But I'm like, it's probably
still okay, I'll just --

00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:30.103
it's going to come out
of the pan, and I turn over

00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:32.004
the Bundt pan to take
the cake out,

00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:34.874
and it's stuck in the Bundt pan.
So now I'm just like livid.

00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:36.775
I'm furious, and I'm like,
[bleep] it,

00:04:36.775 --> 00:04:38.144
I'm throwing it away.

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:40.346
So I start ripping the cake
with my hands.

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:42.415
Hot cake, with my hands,
out of the Bundt pan

00:04:42.415 --> 00:04:44.217
and throw it in the garbage.

00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:46.285
But I took a fistful of it.

00:04:46.285 --> 00:04:48.754
Like, it's equivalent to maybe
a piece or two,

00:04:48.754 --> 00:04:50.456
and just start shoving
it in my mouth,

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:51.924
and they're
throwing the rest away,

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:55.127
shoving the hot cake in my
mouth, which was terrific.

00:04:55.127 --> 00:04:57.730
So -- But the problem was,
I got all the food shame

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:58.931
without the pride.

00:04:58.931 --> 00:05:00.666
I screwed up the cake,

00:05:00.666 --> 00:05:03.035
and was just angry at myself,
and I was eating it

00:05:03.035 --> 00:05:05.638
by the handful, which made me
feel like a pig.

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:08.708
And so there was no joy.
Very little joy.

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:10.909
-Yeah. No.
-Just shame and fury.

00:05:10.909 --> 00:05:13.012
But, a week later, I nailed it.

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:15.347
Made the cake,
and it came out perfect.

00:05:15.347 --> 00:05:16.949
But then, the other problem is,

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:18.917
I was like very proud
of my cake, but I live alone.

00:05:18.917 --> 00:05:21.487
So, now I got to figure out how
to unload the cake.

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:24.223
So, I'm out in the street
with my mask on.

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:28.194
My neighbor's cleaning his car,
I'm like, "Troy, you want cake?

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:30.629
How about some cake?"
And Troy's like, "Yeah."

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:32.731
So, I go in the house,
I bring Troy some cake,

00:05:32.731 --> 00:05:34.500
and then I give it to him,
and that puts you

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.436
in a weird situation,
'cause two hours later

00:05:36.436 --> 00:05:38.838
I'm kind of hanging around
outside looking for Troy, like,

00:05:38.838 --> 00:05:41.941
"Hey, how was the cake?
Cake pretty good?"

00:05:41.941 --> 00:05:43.342
And then he's like,
"It's a little sweet."

00:05:43.342 --> 00:05:44.810
And I'm like,
"What the [bleep] do you want?

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:46.913
It was free cake."
I didn't say that part out loud.

00:05:46.913 --> 00:05:48.547
But that's what I was thinking
about it.

00:05:48.547 --> 00:05:50.483
-Don't judge.
You just say it was good

00:05:50.483 --> 00:05:52.218
and you go back inside.

00:05:52.218 --> 00:05:53.953
-But then he said as I'm
walking away, he goes,

00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:55.187
"You should open a bakery."

00:05:55.187 --> 00:05:56.955
So I guess this is
the announcement.

00:05:56.955 --> 00:05:59.057
I'm opening a bakery.
-This is not true.

00:05:59.057 --> 00:06:00.760
Before we get into your bakery,

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:02.728
I want to talk about
"Stardust."

00:06:02.728 --> 00:06:05.030
Because I'm very excited
about this.

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:07.400
I'm very, very
excited about this.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.635
I love David Bowie.
-Yeah.

00:06:09.635 --> 00:06:13.806
-And this is a story -- a very
specific time in bowie's life.

00:06:13.806 --> 00:06:15.375
-Right, this is getting --

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:16.909
People are going, like,
"It's a biopic."

00:06:16.909 --> 00:06:18.878
It's not a biopic.
Yeah, it's very --

00:06:18.878 --> 00:06:21.013
It's a -- Why am I holding
a piece of paper towel

00:06:21.013 --> 00:06:24.350
on national television?
Because I'm outside my house!

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:28.855
[ Laughter ]

00:06:28.855 --> 00:06:30.923
There's no mystery to this
anymore, Jimmy.

00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:33.125
-No!
-I saw your couch last week.

00:06:33.125 --> 00:06:35.494
You had to go back to the studio
because your wife was like,

00:06:35.494 --> 00:06:37.763
"Can we not do this in the house
anymore?

00:06:37.763 --> 00:06:40.499
Does this have to be done
in the house, Jimmy?

00:06:40.499 --> 00:06:43.602
I can't keep the kids quiet
for an hour, Jimmy."

00:06:43.602 --> 00:06:45.872
-"Get back to work."

00:06:45.872 --> 00:06:48.007
Yeah, exactly right, buddy.

00:06:48.007 --> 00:06:51.210
-I'm making a bread, Jimmy!
-Exactly right.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.512
-But this -- I like this --
-Okay, so anyways --

00:06:53.512 --> 00:06:57.049
-I like this because you're the
publicist for Mercury Records.

00:06:57.049 --> 00:07:00.252
-Right. Ron Oberman was
kind of a down-and-out publicist

00:07:00.252 --> 00:07:01.621
for Mercury Records.

00:07:01.621 --> 00:07:05.090
And the time period,
it's before Bowie's a star.

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:07.326
He had a hit with
"Space Oddity,"

00:07:07.326 --> 00:07:10.596
But no one knew whether he was a
novelty act or a one-time thing.

00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:14.366
And he made "The Man Who
Sold the World" and it got --

00:07:14.366 --> 00:07:15.768
People didn't know
what to do with it.

00:07:15.768 --> 00:07:17.403
It was a dark record.
-Yeah.

00:07:17.403 --> 00:07:20.039
-So they kind of send him
to America.

00:07:20.039 --> 00:07:22.207
In the movie, he doesn't have
the right papers.

00:07:22.207 --> 00:07:24.143
I'm the only guy that's
going to champion him.

00:07:24.143 --> 00:07:26.011
The label doesn't know
what to do with it.

00:07:26.011 --> 00:07:29.715
He's about to lose his deal,
but I think he's a genius.

00:07:29.715 --> 00:07:32.118
So it's sort of -- My part
of the movie is him and I --

00:07:32.118 --> 00:07:34.720
I I'm trying to get him
little gigs

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.289
to play or be on the radio
without the proper paperwork

00:07:37.289 --> 00:07:39.525
for him to play, so we
spend a lot of time driving

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:41.160
in the car across country.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:42.862
He sort of doesn't know
who he's going to be

00:07:42.862 --> 00:07:44.496
or what he wants to be.

00:07:44.496 --> 00:07:46.966
And there's all these
conversations about music

00:07:46.966 --> 00:07:49.802
and about whether
he believes in himself.

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:51.937
And there's a back story
about his stepbrother

00:07:51.937 --> 00:07:53.706
who had mental problems,
and he was worried

00:07:53.706 --> 00:07:55.207
he was going to have
mental problems.

00:07:55.207 --> 00:07:59.879
So it's a very small
slice of a young Bowie's life.

00:07:59.879 --> 00:08:02.882
-I love that.
I know you love music.

00:08:02.882 --> 00:08:04.550
So I go --
-Love it.

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:08.020
-Marc is perfect for this
because we always discuss music.

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:10.456
I know you're a giant
Stones fan.

00:08:10.456 --> 00:08:12.724
Me as well.
We just love --

00:08:12.724 --> 00:08:14.193
-Yeah.
-I mean, just mu--

00:08:14.193 --> 00:08:15.794
Do you remember the first time
you heard Bowie?

00:08:15.794 --> 00:08:16.996
-The first time I heard Bowie

00:08:16.996 --> 00:08:20.199
I think was at
a 7th grade dance.

00:08:20.199 --> 00:08:23.869
The song "Fame" was a big hit.
-Whew, wow.

00:08:23.869 --> 00:08:27.707
-And I remember this because
there was a local AM deejay

00:08:27.707 --> 00:08:30.009
named Bobby Box, and you know,
when you're a kid,

00:08:30.009 --> 00:08:31.376
you listen to the radio
all the time

00:08:31.376 --> 00:08:33.179
and Bobby Box was the guy.

00:08:33.179 --> 00:08:38.183
And Bobby Box was coming to
our school to host the dance,

00:08:38.183 --> 00:08:40.352
so we're all excited to
see Bobby Box.

00:08:40.352 --> 00:08:42.855
Turns out,
he's basically a dwarf

00:08:42.855 --> 00:08:47.627
and he was just like a giant
head in a strange plaid suit...

00:08:47.627 --> 00:08:48.994
-Yep.
-And we are like,

00:08:48.994 --> 00:08:50.296
"Oh, man, this is sad."

00:08:50.296 --> 00:08:52.130
-That was real deejays
back then.

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:54.734
-Right, real guys.
But then "Fame" comes on,

00:08:54.734 --> 00:08:58.237
and you know that part, like,
"Fame, fame, fame, fame..."?

00:08:58.237 --> 00:09:00.405
-Yeah, John Lennon.
-Did you know -- Yeah.

00:09:00.405 --> 00:09:02.741
I found out John Lennon was
part of that crew.

00:09:02.741 --> 00:09:04.410
And it's always weird
when you find that out

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:06.878
'cause then you're listening
for the John Lennon,

00:09:06.878 --> 00:09:08.347
for the one John Lennon "fame."

00:09:08.347 --> 00:09:10.215
That's John!
-Yeah, or it's like this,

00:09:10.215 --> 00:09:12.384
like, "The song isn't about him
'cause he's going,

00:09:12.384 --> 00:09:14.487
'Is it about you?'
He's harmon--

00:09:14.487 --> 00:09:17.056
So the song is not -- Yeah,
I love finding that stuff out.

00:09:17.056 --> 00:09:19.425
-Do remember like Velvet --
like Lou Reed,

00:09:19.425 --> 00:09:21.727
"Satellite of Love"?
-Yeah.

00:09:21.727 --> 00:09:23.596
-Listen to that.
It's Bowie in the back.

00:09:23.596 --> 00:09:25.097
Like, 'cause he produced it.

00:09:25.097 --> 00:09:27.132
So, "uh-hoo!"
-Yes.

00:09:27.132 --> 00:09:29.201
-It's like, that's Bowie!

00:09:29.201 --> 00:09:30.836
You didn't know that, Jimmy.
-I didn't know that, dude.

00:09:30.836 --> 00:09:32.271
-You didn't know it.
-You just saw that.

00:09:32.271 --> 00:09:34.774
-And I just told it to you,
and now you're gonna know it.

00:09:34.774 --> 00:09:36.375
-Wow!
-Now you know,

00:09:36.375 --> 00:09:38.210
"Satellite of Love"
by Lou Reed --

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:39.845
listen to it,
it's on "Transformer."

00:09:39.845 --> 00:09:41.447
-Bowie's back there.
-You keep giving me --

00:09:41.447 --> 00:09:43.215
Oh, "Transformer,"
that's unbelievable.

00:09:43.215 --> 00:09:44.550
No way. You just --
-Yeah.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.451
-You keep giving me gifts.
-Go listen to it now.

00:09:46.451 --> 00:09:48.420
-You keep giving me gifts.
-I know.

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:50.623
-The next thing I want is cake.

00:09:50.623 --> 00:09:52.924
-I'll give you cake.
Oh, my God.

00:09:52.924 --> 00:09:55.260
-Dude, stay safe, thank you
so much for coming back

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:56.795
to our show.
I want to show a clip.

00:09:56.795 --> 00:10:00.766
Here is Marc Maron
in "Stardust." Take a look.

00:10:00.766 --> 00:10:02.867
-Jeez, Tom, I have been
calling for weeks,

00:10:02.867 --> 00:10:04.336
looking all over for you.

00:10:04.336 --> 00:10:05.771
Did you get that album,
the David Bowie album?

00:10:05.771 --> 00:10:07.773
Did you listen to it?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:10:07.773 --> 00:10:09.175
-Great.
-I listened to it.

00:10:09.175 --> 00:10:10.742
-Great, when can I get
you two to sit down

00:10:10.742 --> 00:10:12.678
for an interview together?

00:10:12.678 --> 00:10:15.381
-Look, I'm -- Listen,
I'm really busy right now.

00:10:15.381 --> 00:10:18.116
-No, no, you listen, okay,
and listen good, okay?

00:10:18.116 --> 00:10:19.818
You don't want to miss out
on this, man.

00:10:19.818 --> 00:10:21.153
I mean, this guy's
going to be big.

00:10:21.153 --> 00:10:23.589
-Oh, yeah? How big is big?
-Big.

00:10:23.589 --> 00:10:26.025
-What, like Jerry Reed big
or Todd Rundgren big,

00:10:26.025 --> 00:10:28.493
or what are we --
-No, I'm talking like Dylan big.

00:10:28.493 --> 00:10:30.229
-[Bleep], come on.
-Serious.

00:10:30.229 --> 00:10:32.031
This guy's like
the English Bob Dylan

00:10:32.031 --> 00:10:34.467
with avant garde outrage
all wrapped together.

00:10:34.467 --> 00:10:37.236
-What are you, reading this off
the [bleep] press release?

00:10:37.236 --> 00:10:40.005
Marc Maron, everybody.
"Stardust" is in theaters

00:10:40.005 --> 00:10:42.074
and on demand November 25th.

00:10:42.074 --> 00:10:44.509
Marc, thank you so much again
for coming on.

00:10:44.509 --> 00:10:46.912
It's always a pleasure, buddy.
-Thank you for having me.

00:10:46.912 --> 00:10:48.647
It was the easiest
"Tonight Show,"

00:10:48.647 --> 00:10:50.749
I did my own hair and makeup,
wore my own --

00:10:50.749 --> 00:10:52.384
-I'll get you those cookies.
I promise.

00:10:52.384 --> 00:10:53.886
-Thank you, I'm counting on it.

